Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"To achieve a hybrid cloud model, a company must always migrate from a private cloud model.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"A company can extend the capacity of its internal network by using the public cloud.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"In a public cloud model, only guest users at your company can access the resources in the cloud.","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"No","stmt_2":"Yes","stmt_3":"No"}', 'Statement 1: A company can adopt a hybrid cloud model directly without migrating from an existing private cloud. Statement 2: Public cloud can extend on-premises network and compute capacity. Statement 3: In a public cloud model, authorized corporate users, administrators, and services can access resources, not just guest users.', 'after_submission', 3, 1, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:05:34', '2026-10-28 19:05:34') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"To achieve a hybrid cloud model, a company must always migrate from a private cloud model.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"A company can extend the capacity of its internal network by using the public cloud.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"In a public cloud model, only guest users at your company can access the resources in the cloud.","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"No","stmt_2":"Yes","stmt_3":"No"}', 'Box 1: No - It is not true that a company must always migrate from a private cloud model to implement a hybrid cloud. You could start with a public cloud and then combine that with an on-premises infrastructure to implement a hybrid cloud.

Box 2: Yes - A company can extend the capacity of its internal network by using the public cloud. This is very common. When you need more capacity, rather than pay for new on-premises infrastructure, you can configure a cloud environment and connect your on-premises network to the cloud environment by using a VPN.

Box 3: No - It is not true that only guest users can access cloud resources. You can give anyone with an account in Azure Active Directory access to the cloud resources. There are many authentication scenarios, but a common one is to replicate your on-premises Active Directory accounts to Azure Active Directory and provide access to the Azure Active Directory accounts. Another commonly used authentication method is Federation, where authentication for access to cloud resources is passed to another authentication provider such as an on-premises Active Directory.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:08:18', '2026-10-28 19:08:18') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"Azure Pay-As-You-Go pricing is an example of CapEx.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"Paying electricity for your datacenter is an example of OpEx.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"Deploying your own datacenter is an example of CapEx.","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"No","stmt_2":"No","stmt_3":"Yes"}', 'Box 1: No - With the pay-as-you-go model, you pay for services as you use them. This is OpEx (Operational Expenditure), not CapEx (Capital Expenditure). CapEx is where you pay for something upfront, for example, buying a new physical server.

Box 2: No - Paying for electricity for your own datacenter is classed as CapEx, not OpEx, according to the provided source explanation.

Box 3: Yes - Deploying your own datacenter is an example of CapEx. This is because you need to purchase all the infrastructure upfront before you can use it.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:10:19', '2026-10-28 19:10:19') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"With software as a service (SaaS), you must apply software updates.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"With infrastructure as a service (IaaS), you must install the software that you want to use.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"Azure Backup is an example of platform as a service (PaaS).","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"No","stmt_2":"Yes","stmt_3":"Yes"}', 'Statement 1: With software as a service (SaaS), you must apply software updates. Answer: No. Explanation: In a SaaS model, such as Microsoft 365, Salesforce, or Gmail, the cloud provider manages the entire application stack. This includes hardware, virtualization, operating systems, and the application software itself. The vendor automatically handles all software updates, feature upgrades, and bug patches behind the scenes without any effort or intervention from the end user.

Statement 2: With infrastructure as a service (IaaS), you must install the software that you want to use. Answer: Yes. Explanation: IaaS provides the rawest form of cloud computing resources, typically virtual machines, storage, and networking. While the cloud provider manages the physical data center hardware and hypervisor, you are given full administrative control over the operating system. This means you are completely responsible for installing, configuring, and maintaining any software, runtimes, middleware, or applications you intend to run.

Statement 3: Azure Backup is an example of platform as a service (PaaS). Answer: Yes. Explanation: PaaS provides a managed environment where you can deploy applications or consume services.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:14:36', '2026-10-28 19:14:36') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"Microsoft SQL Server 2019 installed on an Azure virtual machine is an example of platform as a service (PaaS).","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"Azure SQL Database is an example of platform as a service (PaaS).","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"Azure Cosmos DB is an example of software as a service (SaaS).","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"No","stmt_2":"Yes","stmt_3":"No"}', 'Statement 1: Microsoft SQL Server 2019 installed on an Azure virtual machine is an example of platform as a service (PaaS). Answer: No. Explanation: When you deploy an Azure Virtual Machine and install SQL Server on it, you are leveraging Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS). You are responsible for managing the underlying operating system, configuring the SQL Server installation, managing storage disks, and applying both OS patches and database cumulative updates.

Statement 2: Azure SQL Database is an example of platform as a service (PaaS). Answer: Yes. Explanation: Azure SQL Database is a fully managed relational database engine. Azure handles all core infrastructure management, including hardware provisioning, operating system patching, database software updates, backups, and high availability configurations. You only manage your schema, data, and access permissions, which fits the definition of PaaS.

Statement 3: Azure Cosmos DB is an example of software as a service (SaaS). Answer: No. Explanation: Azure Cosmos DB is not classified as SaaS in this question.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:15:45', '2026-10-28 19:15:45') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"Azure Files is an example of infrastructure as a service (IaaS).","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"A DNS server that runs on an Azure virtual machine is an example of platform as a service (PaaS).","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"Microsoft Intune is an example of software as a service (SaaS).","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"No","stmt_2":"No","stmt_3":"Yes"}', 'Statement 1: Azure Files is an example of infrastructure as a service (IaaS). Answer: No. Explanation: Azure Files is actually a managed file storage service, which is considered a part of Platform as a Service (PaaS).

Statement 2: A DNS server that runs on an Azure virtual machine is an example of platform as a service (PaaS). Answer: No. Explanation: A DNS server running on an Azure virtual machine would be considered Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) because it involves managing the underlying infrastructure.

Statement 3: Microsoft Intune is an example of software as a service (SaaS). Answer: Yes. Explanation: Microsoft Intune is indeed a Software as a Service (SaaS) as it provides software over the internet without needing local installation.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:16:36', '2026-10-28 19:16:36') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"Cloud computing provides elastic scalability.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"Customers can minimize capital expenditure (CapEx) by using a public cloud.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"Cloud computing leverages virtualization to provide services to multiple customers simultaneously.","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"Yes","stmt_2":"Yes","stmt_3":"Yes"}', 'Statement 1: Yes - Cloud computing provides elastic scalability. Elasticity means the system can automatically add or remove resources, such as server power or storage, to match current demand.

Statement 2: Yes - Customers can minimize capital expenditure (CapEx) by using a public cloud. Instead of buying expensive hardware upfront, customers pay for what they use as an ongoing operating expense (OpEx).

Statement 3: Yes - Cloud computing leverages virtualization to provide services to multiple customers simultaneously. Virtualization allows a single physical server to be divided into multiple virtual environments, enabling providers to serve many customers using shared underlying hardware.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:17:19', '2026-10-28 19:17:19') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"Azure virtual networks deployed to the same Azure region are connected by default.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"Each Azure virtual network in a single resource group must have a unique name.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"The Azure virtual network''s address space must be unique within a subscription.","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"No","stmt_2":"Yes","stmt_3":"Yes"}', 'Box 1: No - You would need to set up the VNET connections, for example with peering. You can connect virtual networks to each other with virtual network peering. These virtual networks can be in the same region or different regions, also known as Global VNet peering. Once virtual networks are peered, resources in both virtual networks can communicate with each other.

Box 2: Yes - All Azure resource types have a scope that defines the level at which resource names must be unique. A resource must have a unique name within its scope. For example, a virtual network has a resource group scope, which means that there can be only one network named vnet-prod-westus-001 in a given resource group.

Box 3: Yes - As you build your network in Azure, it is important to keep in mind the following universal design principles: Ensure non-overlapping address spaces. Make sure your VNet address space (CIDR block) does not overlap with your organization''s other network ranges.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:18:05', '2026-10-28 19:18:05') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"An Azure subscription can be associated to multiple Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) tenants.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"You can change the Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) tenant to which an Azure subscription is associated.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"When an Azure subscription expires, the associated Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) tenant is deleted automatically.","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"No","stmt_2":"Yes","stmt_3":"No"}', 'Statement 1: No - An Azure subscription is associated with a single Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) tenant at a time.

Statement 2: Yes - You can change the Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) tenant to which an Azure subscription is associated.

Statement 3: No - The Azure Active Directory (Azure AD) tenant is not automatically deleted when an associated Azure subscription expires.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:18:49', '2026-10-28 19:18:49') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"Each Azure subscription can contain multiple account administrators.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"Each Azure subscription can be managed by using a Microsoft account only.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"An Azure resource group contains multiple Azure subscriptions.","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"No","stmt_2":"No","stmt_3":"No"}', 'Box 1: No - You can assign service administrators and co-administrators in the Azure portal, but there can only be one account administrator.

Box 2: No - You need an Azure Active Directory account to manage a subscription, not a Microsoft account only. An account is created in Azure Active Directory when you create the subscription. Further accounts can be created in Azure Active Directory to manage the subscription.

Box 3: No - Resource groups are logical containers for Azure resources. However, resource groups do not contain subscriptions. Subscriptions contain resource groups.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:19:41', '2026-10-28 19:19:41') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"Data that is stored in an Azure Storage account automatically has at least three copies.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"All data that is copied to an Azure Storage account is backed up automatically to another Azure data center.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"An Azure Storage account can contain up to 2 TB of data and up to one million files.","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"Yes","stmt_2":"No","stmt_3":"No"}', 'Box 1: Yes - There are different replication options available with a storage account. The minimum replication option is Locally Redundant Storage (LRS). With LRS, data is replicated synchronously three times within the primary region.

Box 2: No - Data is not backed up automatically to another Azure data center in every storage configuration. This depends on the replication option configured for the storage account. Locally Redundant Storage (LRS) maintains three copies of the data locally, while geo-redundant storage options replicate data to a secondary region.

Box 3: No - The limits are much higher than 2 TB of data and one million files. Therefore, the statement is incorrect.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:20:42', '2026-10-28 19:20:42') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"You can add an Azure Resource Manager template to an Azure blueprint.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"You can assign an Azure blueprint to a resource group.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"You can use Azure Blueprints to grant permissions to a resource.","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"Yes","stmt_2":"No","stmt_3":"Yes"}', 'Box 1: Yes - Each blueprint can consist of zero or more ARM template artifacts. Azure Resource Manager templates can therefore be included in an Azure blueprint.

Box 2: No - To create a new resource group for use by other artifacts within the blueprint, a resource group artifact can be added. Blueprints can be saved to a management group or subscription. A blueprint can then be assigned to a child subscription of that management group. Each published version of a blueprint can be assigned, with a maximum length of 90 characters, to an existing management group or subscription.

Box 3: Yes - The blueprint definition permissions must be granted or inherited on the management group or subscription scope where it is saved. This enables permissions to be applied to resources.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:22:28', '2026-10-28 19:22:28') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;
Hot Area:
Answer Area', '[{"id":"stmt_1","statement":"You can deploy Azure Resource Manager (ARM) templates by using the Azure portal.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_2","statement":"Azure Resource Manager (ARM) templates can define infrastructure by using code.","options":["Yes","No"]},{"id":"stmt_3","statement":"Each Azure resource to be deployed requires a separate Azure Resource Manager (ARM) template.","options":["Yes","No"]}]', '{"stmt_1":"Yes","stmt_2":"Yes","stmt_3":"No"}', 'Box 1: Yes - You can deploy Azure Resource Manager (ARM) templates by using the Azure portal.

Box 2: Yes - Azure Resource Manager (ARM) templates can define infrastructure by using code.

Box 3: No - It is not necessary to use a separate template for each resource. For example, an ARM template can define a virtual machine, a storage account, and a virtual network together in a single template.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:23:50', '2026-10-28 19:23:50') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;

NOTE: Each correct match is worth one point.', '{"source_title":"Storage service","target_title":"Answer Area","sources":[{"id":"src_blob","text":"Azure Blob storage"},{"id":"src_disk","text":"Azure Disk storage"},{"id":"src_files","text":"Azure Files"},{"id":"src_queue","text":"Azure Queue Storage"}],"targets":[{"id":"tgt_msg","text":"Used for reliable messaging between applications"},{"id":"tgt_share","text":"Can be accessed as a network share from a Windows device"},{"id":"tgt_archive","text":"Can be configured to use the Archive access tier"}]}', '{"tgt_msg":"src_queue","tgt_share":"src_files","tgt_archive":"src_blob"}', 'Azure Queue Storage is designed for asynchronous, reliable message queues between application components. Azure Files provides serverless SMB/NFS file shares mounted directly on Windows, macOS, or Linux. Azure Blob storage supports the Archive access tier for low-cost offline data storage. Azure Disk storage is persistent block storage attached to virtual machines.', 'after_submission', 3, 1, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:42:01', '2026-10-28 19:42:01') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;

NOTE: Each correct match is worth one point.', '{"source_title":"Benefits","target_title":"Answer Area","sources":[{"id":"src_agility","text":"Agility"},{"id":"src_elasticity","text":"Elasticity"},{"id":"src_disaster_recovery","text":"Disaster recovery"}],"targets":[{"id":"tgt_scale","text":"The ability to dynamically scale the resources available to a cloud app"},{"id":"tgt_restore","text":"The ability to use cloud-based backup services to restore resources in the event of an outage"},{"id":"tgt_deploy","text":"The ability to quickly deploy and configure cloud-based resources as app requirements change"}]}', '{"tgt_scale":"src_elasticity","tgt_restore":"src_disaster_recovery","tgt_deploy":"src_agility"}', 'Elasticity matches the ability to dynamically scale the resources available to a cloud app. Elasticity is the ability of a system to automatically commission and decommission resources such as RAM, CPU, or virtual machines to match current demand closely.

Disaster recovery matches the ability to use cloud-based backup services to restore resources in the event of an outage.

Agility matches the ability to quickly deploy and configure cloud-based resources as application requirements change.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:48:17', '2026-10-28 19:48:17') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;

NOTE: Each correct match is worth one point.', '{"source_title":"Answer Options","target_title":"Answer Area","sources":[{"id":"src_azure_government","text":"Azure Government"},{"id":"src_gdpr","text":"GDPR"},{"id":"src_iso","text":"ISO"},{"id":"src_nist","text":"NIST"}],"targets":[{"id":"tgt_international_standards","text":"An organization that defines international standards across all industries."},{"id":"tgt_us_standards","text":"An organization that defines standards used by the United States government."},{"id":"tgt_european_privacy","text":"A European policy that regulates data privacy and data protection."},{"id":"tgt_us_government_cloud","text":"A dedicated public cloud for federal and state agencies in the United States."}]}', '{"tgt_international_standards":"src_iso","tgt_us_standards":"src_nist","tgt_european_privacy":"src_gdpr","tgt_us_government_cloud":"src_azure_government"}', 'Box 1: ISO - ISO is the International Organization for Standardization. It defines international standards across industries.

Box 2: NIST - NIST is the National Institute of Standards and Technology and defines standards used by the United States government.

Box 3: GDPR - GDPR stands for General Data Protection Regulation and regulates data privacy and data protection in Europe.

Box 4: Azure Government - Azure Government is a dedicated cloud environment designed for United States government organizations.', 'after_submission', 4, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:49:38', '2026-10-28 19:49:38') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;

NOTE: Each correct match is worth one point.', '{"source_title":"Azure Options","target_title":"Answer Area","sources":[{"id":"src_advisor","text":"Azure Advisor"},{"id":"src_cognitive_services","text":"Azure Cognitive Services"},{"id":"src_app_insights","text":"Azure Application Insights"},{"id":"src_devops","text":"Azure DevOps"}],"targets":[{"id":"tgt_development","text":"An integrated solution for the deployment of code"},{"id":"tgt_recommendations","text":"A tool that provides guidance and recommendations to improve an Azure environment"},{"id":"tgt_ai","text":"A simplified tool to build intelligent Artificial Intelligence (AI) applications"},{"id":"tgt_monitor","text":"Monitors web applications"}]}', '{"tgt_development":"src_devops","tgt_recommendations":"src_advisor","tgt_ai":"src_cognitive_services","tgt_monitor":"src_app_insights"}', 'Box 1: Azure DevOps - Azure DevOps is Microsoft''s primary software development and deployment platform. Azure DevOps influences the application lifecycle throughout its plan, develop, deliver, and operate phases.

Box 2: Azure Advisor - Azure Advisor is a personalized cloud consultant that helps you follow best practices to optimize your Azure deployments. It analyzes resource configuration and usage telemetry and then recommends solutions that can help improve cost effectiveness, performance, high availability, and security of your Azure resources.

Box 3: Azure Cognitive Services - Azure Cognitive Services provides APIs, SDKs, and services designed to help developers build intelligent applications without requiring direct AI or data science skills or knowledge.

Box 4: Azure Application Insights - Azure Application Insights detects and diagnoses anomalies in web applications.', 'after_submission', 4, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:50:46', '2026-10-28 19:50:46') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;

NOTE: Each correct match is worth one point.', '{"source_title":"Models","target_title":"Answer Area","sources":[{"id":"src_iaas","text":"Infrastructure as a service (IaaS)"},{"id":"src_paas","text":"Platform as a service (PaaS)"},{"id":"src_saas","text":"Software as a service (SaaS)"}],"targets":[{"id":"tgt_app_service","text":"Azure App Service"},{"id":"tgt_virtual_machines","text":"Azure virtual machines"},{"id":"tgt_dynamics_365","text":"Microsoft Dynamics 365"}]}', '{"tgt_app_service":"src_paas","tgt_virtual_machines":"src_iaas","tgt_dynamics_365":"src_saas"}', 'Box 1: Platform as a service (PaaS) - Azure App Service is a fully managed platform for building web applications. Platform as a service provides a complete development and deployment environment in the cloud with resources that enable you to deliver applications.

Box 2: Infrastructure as a service (IaaS) - Azure virtual machines are an Infrastructure as a Service offering in Azure.

Box 3: Software as a service (SaaS) - Microsoft Dynamics 365 is a set of interconnected, modular Software as a Service applications and services.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:51:41', '2026-10-28 19:51:41') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;

NOTE: Each correct match is worth one point.', '{"source_title":"Services","target_title":"Answer Area","sources":[{"id":"src_sphere","text":"Azure Sphere"},{"id":"src_iot_central","text":"IoT Central"},{"id":"src_iot_hub","text":"IoT Hub"}],"targets":[{"id":"tgt_bidirectional","text":"A managed service that provides bidirectional communication between IoT devices and Azure"},{"id":"tgt_saas","text":"A fully managed software as a service (SaaS) solution to connect, monitor, and manage IoT devices at scale"},{"id":"tgt_security","text":"A software and hardware solution that provides communication and security features for IoT devices"}]}', '{"tgt_bidirectional":"src_iot_hub","tgt_saas":"src_iot_central","tgt_security":"src_sphere"}', 'Box 1: IoT Hub - IoT Hub is a managed service that provides bidirectional communication between IoT devices and Azure. It acts as a central message hub for secure bidirectional communication between IoT applications and the devices they manage.

Box 2: IoT Central - IoT Central is a fully managed software as a service (SaaS) solution used to connect, monitor, and manage IoT devices at scale.

Box 3: Azure Sphere - Azure Sphere is a software and hardware solution that provides communication and security features for IoT devices.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:52:51', '2026-10-28 19:52:51') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;

NOTE: Each correct match is worth one point.', '{"source_title":"Answer Options","target_title":"Answer Area","sources":[{"id":"src_disaster_recovery","text":"Disaster recovery"},{"id":"src_fault_tolerance","text":"Fault tolerance"},{"id":"src_low_latency","text":"Low latency"},{"id":"src_dynamic_scalability","text":"Dynamic scalability"}],"targets":[{"id":"tgt_available_failure","text":"A cloud service that remains available after a failure occurs"},{"id":"tgt_recovered_failure","text":"A cloud service that can be recovered after a failure occurs"},{"id":"tgt_demand","text":"A cloud service that performs quickly when demand increases"},{"id":"tgt_internet","text":"A cloud service that can be accessed quickly from the Internet"}]}', '{"tgt_available_failure":"src_fault_tolerance","tgt_recovered_failure":"src_disaster_recovery","tgt_demand":"src_dynamic_scalability","tgt_internet":"src_low_latency"}', 'Box 1: Fault tolerance - Fault tolerance is the ability of a service to remain available after a failure of one of the components of the service. For example, a service running on multiple servers can withstand the failure of one of the servers.

Box 2: Disaster recovery - Disaster recovery is the recovery of a service after a failure. For example, restoring a virtual machine from backup after a virtual machine failure.

Box 3: Dynamic scalability - Dynamic scalability is the ability for compute resources to be added to a service when the service is under heavy load. For example, in a virtual machine scale set, additional instances of the virtual machine are added when the existing virtual machines are under heavy load.

Box 4: Low latency - Latency is the time a service takes to respond to requests. For example, the time it takes for a web page to be returned from a web server. Low latency means a quicker response.', 'after_submission', 4, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:56:44', '2026-10-28 19:56:44') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;

NOTE: Each correct match is worth one point.', '{"source_title":"Cloud model","target_title":"Work Area","sources":[{"id":"src_hybrid_cloud","text":"Hybrid Cloud"},{"id":"src_private_cloud","text":"Private Cloud"},{"id":"src_public_cloud","text":"Public Cloud"}],"targets":[{"id":"tgt_no_capex","text":"No required capital expenditure."},{"id":"tgt_security_control","text":"Provides complete control over security."},{"id":"tgt_choice","text":"Provides a choice to use on-premises or cloud-based resources."}]}', '{"tgt_no_capex":"src_public_cloud","tgt_security_control":"src_private_cloud","tgt_choice":"src_hybrid_cloud"}', 'Box 1: Public Cloud - With a public cloud, there is no required capital expenditure on server hardware. You pay for the cloud resources that you use as you use them.

Box 2: Private Cloud - A private cloud can exist on-premises, providing the organization with control over its security.

Box 3: Hybrid Cloud - A hybrid cloud combines public cloud resources and on-premises resources. Therefore, an organization has the choice to use on-premises or cloud-based resources.', 'after_submission', 3, 0, 0, 1, 0, 'published', 1, NULL, 'usr_admin_001', '2026-10-28 19:58:05', '2026-10-28 19:58:05') ON CONFLICT ("id") DO NOTHING;

NOTE: Each correct match is worth one point.', '{"source_title":"Benefits","target_title":"Answer Area","sources":[{"id":"src_agility","text":"Agility"},{"id":"src_geo_distribution","text":"Geo-distribution"},{"id":"src_scalability","text":"Scalability"}],"targets":[{"id":"tgt_dynamic_resources","text":"Resources can be provisioned dynamically to meet changing demands."},{"id":"tgt_multiple_regions","text":"Applications and data can be deployed to multiple regions."},{"id":"tgt_rapid_development","text":"Applications can be developed, tested, and launched rapidly."}]}', '{"tgt_dynamic_resources":"src_scalability","tgt_multiple_regions":"src_geo_distribution","tgt_rapid_development":"src_agility"}', 'Box 1: Scalability - Resources can be provisioned dynamically to meet changing demands. Scalability refers to a system''s capacity to handle growing or shrinking workloads by adjusting its resource footprint. This can happen horizontally by adding or removing instances, or vertically by upgrading or downgrading the resources of an existing instance.

Box 2: Geo-distribution - Applications and data can be deployed to multiple regions. Geo-distribution involves running application instances and replicating data stores across distinct physical datacenter locations globally. This can improve regional redundancy and reduce network latency for geographically distributed users.

